<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">3169264</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>1988</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>14</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2019</Year><Month>06</Month><Day>21</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">0014-5793</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>238</Volume><Issue>2</Issue><PubDate><Year>1988</Year><Month>Oct</Month><Day>10</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>FEBS letters</Title><ISOAbbreviation>FEBS Lett</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Stimulation of collagen synthesis in fibroblast cultures by the tripeptide-copper complex glycyl-L-histidyl-L-lysine-Cu2+.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>343</StartPage><EndPage>346</EndPage><MedlinePgn>343-6</MedlinePgn></Pagination><Abstract><AbstractText>Glycyl-L-histidyl-L-lysine (GHK) is a tripeptide with affinity for copper(II) ions and was isolated from human plasma. This peptide appears to play a physiological role in wound healing. We report the stimulating effect of GHK-Cu on collagen synthesis by fibroblasts. The stimulation began between 10(-12) and 10(-11) M, maximized at 10(-9) M, and was independent of any change in cell number. The presence of a GHK triplet in the alpha 2(I) chain of type I collagen suggests that the tripeptide might be liberated by proteases at the site of a wound and exert in situ healing effects.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Maquart</LastName><ForeName>F X</ForeName><Initials>FX</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratoire de Biochimie, CNRS URA 84, Facult&#xe9; de M&#xe9;decine, Reims, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Pickart</LastName><ForeName>L</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Laurent</LastName><ForeName>M</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gillery</LastName><ForeNam